The '''2021 UEFA Europa League final''' was the final match of the [[2020–21 UEFA Europa League]], the 50th season of [[Europe]]'s secondary club [[association football|football]] tournament organised by [[UEFA]], and the 12th season since it was renamed from the UEFA Cup to the [[UEFA Europa League]]. It was played on 26 May 2021 at the [[Gdańsk Stadium|Stadion Gdańsk]] in [[Gdańsk]], Poland, between Spanish club [[Villarreal CF|Villarreal]] and English club [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]].

The final was originally scheduled to be played at the [[Ramón Sánchez Pizjuán Stadium|Estadio Ramón Sánchez Pizjuán]] in [[Seville]], Spain. However, due to the postponement and relocation of the [[2020 UEFA Europa League final|2020 final]] to Cologne, the final hosts were shifted back a year, with Gdańsk instead hosting the 2021 final, as a result of the [[COVID-19 pandemic in Europe]].<ref name="Exco17June">{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/025e-0fb6101cfdbf-8b878372d79d-1000--uefa-competitions-to-resume-in-august/ |title=UEFA competitions to resume in August |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=17 June 2020 |access-date=17 June 2020}}</ref> A capacity limit of 25% was agreed for the 41,620-seater [[Gdańsk Stadium|Stadion Gdańsk]], resulting in an attendance of 9,412.<ref>{{Cite web |last=UEFA.com |date=26 May 2021 |title=All you need to know: 2021 Europa League final |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0269-12303551db2d-57a593f090ff-1000--all-you-need-to-know-2021-europa-league-final/ |access-date=16 June 2026 |website=UEFA.com|language=en}}</ref>

Villarreal won the match 11–10 on penalties following a 1–1 draw after [[Overtime (sports)#Association football|extra time]], thus winning its first trophy in the competition.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0269-125f1aaaeb0d-c59e8a06ac14-1000--villarreal-1-1-manchester-united-aet-11-10-pens-spanish-side/ |title=Villarreal 1-1 Manchester United (aet, 11-10 pens): Spanish side win Europa League in marathon shoot-out |website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=26 May 2021 |access-date=26 May 2021}}</ref> As winners, Villarreal earned the right to play against the winners of the [[2020–21 UEFA Champions League]], [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]], in the [[2021 UEFA Super Cup]]. They also qualified for the group stage of the [[2021–22 UEFA Champions League]].<ref name="uefa1819">{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0242-0e16a85014cb-5ffe48223b95-1000--champions-league-and-europa-league-changes-next-season/ |title=Champions League and Europa League changes next season |website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=27 February 2018 |access-date=27 February 2018}}</ref>

==Venue==
[[File:Stadion PGE Gdańsk - panoramio.jpg|thumb|250x250px|The [[Stadion Gdańsk]] in [[Gdańsk]] hosted the final.]]
The match was played at the [[Stadion Gdańsk]] in [[Gdańsk]], Poland. At the meeting of the UEFA Executive Committee in Kyiv in May 2018, the stadium was initially selected to host the [[2020 UEFA Europa League final]],<ref>{{cite news |title=Istanbul to host 2020 UEFA Champions League Final |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/0245-0f8e60d11f0f-78765b792753-1000--istanbul-to-host-2020-uefa-champions-league-final/ |website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=24 May 2018 |access-date=2 February 2021 }}</ref> but when the COVID-19 pandemic forced UEFA to move the latter stages of the [[2019–20 UEFA Europa League|2019–20 competition]] to Germany, the UEFA Executive Committee delayed [[Gdańsk Stadium]]'s hosting of the Europa League final to 2021. The original venues for the 2021 and [[2022 UEFA Europa League final|2022 finals]] were also delayed by a year.<ref>{{cite news |title=Europa League to resume on 5 August, final on 21 August |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/025e-0f9a414021a7-00e4ef531ed6-1000/ |website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=17 June 2020 |access-date=2 February 2021 }}</ref>

This was the first UEFA club match hosted at the stadium, which was one of the venues for [[UEFA Euro 2012]].<ref name=":0">{{cite news |title=2021 UEFA Europa League final: Gdańsk |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0245-0e98d40b6826-86b29d622244-1000--2021-final-gdansk/ |website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |access-date=2 February 2021 }}</ref> It was the second UEFA club competition final to be played in Poland, as the [[2015 UEFA Europa League final]] was played at the [[Kazimierz Górski National Stadium|National Stadium]] in [[Warsaw]]. When the stadium was selected to host the 2020 final, it was known as Stadion Energa Gdańsk, so due to UEFA sponsorship regulations, marketing materials for the match referred to the venue as Gdańsk Stadium.<ref name="el20"/> Although the sponsorship deal with Energa was ended in November 2020, the stadium continued to be known as Gdańsk Stadium for the final.<ref name=":0"/>

===Host selection===
{{for|the details on the selection of Seville as the original host for the final|2022 UEFA Europa League final#Host selection}}

An open bidding process was launched on 22 September 2017 by UEFA to select the venues of the finals of the UEFA Champions League, UEFA Europa League, and UEFA Women's Champions League in 2020. Associations had until 31 October 2017 to express interest, and bid dossiers had to be submitted by 1 March 2018. Associations hosting matches at [[UEFA Euro 2020]] were not allowed to bid for the 2020 UEFA Europa League final.

UEFA announced on 3 November 2017 that two associations had expressed interest in hosting the 2020 UEFA Europa League final.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/insideuefa/mediaservices/mediareleases/newsid=2516602.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171106015154/http://www.uefa.com/insideuefa/mediaservices/mediareleases/newsid=2516602.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=6 November 2017 |title=Six associations interested in hosting 2020 club finals |website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations|date=3 November 2017}}</ref>

